Description

During security assessment, the application was observed returning verbose error messages when malformed XML input was supplied to the search functionality. The application disclosed internal parser exceptions and processing details:

ERROR org.xml.sax.SAXParseException;
lineNumber:22; columnNumber:44;
The processing instruction target matching "[xX][mM][lL]" is not allowed.

Further interaction with the desktop application resulted in disclosure of internal file system paths:

/SysConfig/Research2/SiteConfig/01_DigitalDesign/PageAccessories/AccessoriesConfig/04_Update-English...

The application exposes implementation details, XML parser information, and internal directory structures to authenticated users. Such information may assist attackers in understanding the backend architecture and facilitate further attacks, including XML-related attacks, Local File Inclusion (LFI), path traversal, or targeted exploitation.

Although XXE payloads were rejected by the parser, the application returned excessive diagnostic information instead of generic error messages.

Impact

An attacker may be able to:

Enumerate internal application components and directory structures.
Identify XML parser technologies and exception handling behavior.
Gain insight into file locations and configuration paths.
Use disclosed information to facilitate path traversal, XXE, or local file inclusion attacks.
Obtain information useful for further reconnaissance and exploitation.
Increase the attack surface by leveraging implementation details.
Mitigation
Configure XML parsers to suppress verbose exception messages.
Return generic error messages to end users.
Log detailed exceptions only on the server side.
Implement centralized exception handling mechanisms.
Avoid exposing internal file names, stack traces, parser details, or directory structures.
Validate and sanitize user-supplied XML input before processing.
Disable dangerous XML features such as external entity resolution unless explicitly required.
Review application configurations and remove sensitive path information from client responses.
